import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class SubstringReplacer {

    public static void replaceSubstrings(List<String> inputList, String searchStr, String replaceStr, String additionalStr) {
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(searchStr);
        for (int i = 0; i < inputList.size(); i++) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(inputList.get(i));
            sb.replaceAll(pattern.pattern(), replaceStr);
            inputList.set(i, sb.toString());
        }
        if (!inputList.contains(additionalStr)) {
            inputList.add(additionalStr);
        }
    }
}
```

This solution uses a regular expression (Pattern and Pattern.compile()) for efficient substring searching, which can provide linear time complexity O(n) in the worst case scenario (when the searched substring is not present). The StringBuilder class is used to build and replace strings efficiently without creating unnecessary intermediate objects. By iterating through the list directly (for loop) instead of using a stream-based approach (e.g., forEach() or map()), we minimize the overhead associated with streaming and provide better performance for large lists.

In cases where performance is critical, consider benchmarking various approaches to find the most efficient solution. Additionally, keep in mind that when dealing with massive data structures, external libraries like Google's Guava StringUtil library may offer optimized string manipulation methods (e.g., Strings.replaceInPlace())."]

