The choice of data structure (linked list) is optimal under expected load conditions due to its ability to handle dynamic data sizes efficiently while maintaining low constant time complexity for common operations like insertion and removal. The use of a doubly-linked list also allows for easy traversal from both ends, which can help in maintaining the order of the elements and optimizing performance when dealing with large datasets.

The trade-offs between memory usage and cache locality are considered by using proper locking mechanisms to minimize cache thrashing caused by frequent mutex acquisition and release. This ensures efficient use of system resources without compromising on network throughput or causing significant delays in the processing of link metrics.

Finally, this implementation accounts for potential network fluctuations and adapts to varying traffic patterns by always using the latest available metric when requested. The function can be extended to provide additional features such as automatic re-initialization of links based on specific thresholds or conditions.']

["Here is a simplified version of the code using C++11 std::unordered_map, pthreads, and a custom Session class for session management. I've also used Boost.Asio's io_service for handling asynchronous operations with an external session persistence library (assuming you have `boost/asio` and `boost/thread` headers included).

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <boost/thread.hpp>
#include <time.h>
#include <cstdlib> // for rand, srand

class Session {
public:
    std::string id;
    std::string auth_token;
    time_t last_activity;
    int timeout_duration;
    bool active;

    Session(std::string id, std::string auth_token, int timeout)
        : id(id), auth_token(auth_token), last_activity(time(0)),
          timeout_duration(timeout), active(true) {}
};

std::unordered_map<std::string, Session*> sessions; // global hash table
std::mutex sessions_mutex; // for thread safety

void create_session(const std::string& id, const std::string& auth_token, int timeout) {
    Session* new_session = new Session(id, auth_token, timeout);
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(sessions_mutex);
        sessions[new_session->id] = new_session;
    }
}

Session* get_session(const std::string& id) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(sessions_mutex);
    auto iter = sessions.find(id);
    if (iter != sessions.end())
        return iter->second;
    return nullptr;
}

void remove_session(const std::string& id) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(sessions_mutex);
    auto iter = sessions.find(id);
    if (iter != sessions.end()) {
        delete iter->second;
        sessions.erase(iter);
    }
}

void session_timeout_handler() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(sessions_mutex);
    auto now = time(0);
    for (auto& pair : sessions) {
        if ((pair.second->last_activity + std::abs(pair.second->timeout_duration)) < now) {
            delete pair.second;
            sessions.erase(pair);
        }
    }
}

// Assuming you have an external persistence library's save() and load() functions
void save_sessions() {
    // Synchronous mode, replace with asynchronous implementation if needed
    for (auto& pair : sessions)
        save(pair.second->id, pair.second);
}

void load_sessions() {
    // Synchronous mode, replace with asynchronous implementation if needed
    for (auto saved_session = load(); saved_session; ) {
        create_session(saved_session->id, saved_session->auth_token, saved_session->timeout_duration);
        saved_session = load();
    }
}

// Replace this with your io_service implementation
boost::asio::io_service io_service;
```

This example provides a basic structure for managing sessions in a concurrent web application. The provided code demonstrates the use of dynamic memory allocation, hash table operations, and thread safety through the use of mutexes. However, you will need to implement your external session persistence library's load() and save() functions as well as a method for handling asynchronous I/O tasks using boost::asio's io_service."]

