[' ```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.math.MathContext;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;

public class MathUtil {
    private static final String FACTORIAL_API = "https://factorial-api.example.com/v1/factorial";
    private static final int MAX_INPUT = 1000;
    private static final MathContext HIGH_PRECISION = new MathContext(256);

    public static BigInteger factorial(BigInteger n) {
        if (n.compareTo(BigInteger.valueOf(MAX_INPUT)) > 0) {
            try {
                HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
                URI uri = new URI(FACTORIAL_API + "?n=" + n);
                HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                    .uri(uri)
                    .build();
                HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofStrings());
                return new BigInteger(response.body(), HIGH_PRECISION);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.err.println("Error occurred while communicating with the external service: " + e.getMessage());
                return null;
            }
        } else {
            BigInteger result = BigInteger.ONE;
            for (BigInteger i = BigInteger.ONE; i.compareTo(n) <= 0; i = i.add(BigInteger.ONE)) {
                result = result.multiply(i);
            }
            return result;
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a `MathUtil` class with a static method called `factorial`. The `factorial` method takes a `BigInteger` as input and returns the factorial of that number as another `BigInteger`. If the input exceeds 1000, it utilizes an external API to calculate the factorial. This is done using Java's HttpClient to send a GET request to the specified API URL with the desired factorial as a query parameter.

The code handles exceptions gracefully by printing an error message and returning null if an error occurs during communication with the external service. The method also ensures high precision for very large numbers by using a `MathContext` instance. Lastly, this implementation is easily extendable for future mathematical operations like prime factorization by creating additional methods within the same class without altering the core functionality of the `factorial` method.']

